BODY = '''<h2>The CPSC Rule for Adult Bed Rails</h2>
<p>Since August 21, 2023, adult portable bed rails made or imported for sale in the U.S. must meet a mandatory CPSC standard, 16 CFR part 1270, which adopts the industry standard ASTM F3186-17 with changes by CPSC. CPSC defines an adult portable bed rail as "an adjacent type bed rail, grab bar, assistive bar, transfer aid, cane or rail" used on, against or next to an adult bed. A product that people are likely to use that way can be in scope even if the maker did not intend it.</p>
<p>The rule tests for entrapment, which CPSC describes as areas, gaps or openings that can capture or restrain a body part. It tests four zones, and it also sets requirements for how the rail is held in place, how strong it is, openings, assembly mistakes, sharp points and edges, and the labels and warnings. Hospital bed guardrails regulated by the FDA and children's bed rails are outside this rule.</p>
<p>CPSC's page gives no death or injury figures and no advice for buyers. If a rail is unsafe, report it at saferproducts.gov. The CDC says more than 1 in 4 older adults falls each year, and a rail is one tool for getting out of bed safely.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>Check the label, not just the listing.</strong> The standard applies to rails made after August 21, 2023. Look on the rail for the maker's compliance statement and a date of manufacture, and ask the seller for the General Certificate of Compliance, which makers and importers must issue. Check the gap between the rail and the mattress after you install it.</p>

<h2>How to Choose a Bed Assist Rail</h2>
<h3>Named standard</h3>
<p>Only the Stander listing names ASTM F3186-17, the standard CPSC adopted. The KingPavonini says ASTM without a number, the Medical King says "USA safety requirements", and the Medline and Lunderg listings name nothing. A claim you cannot check is a reason to ask.</p>
<h3>Handle or longer rail</h3>
<p>A short handle, like the Medline's, helps you sit up and stand. A longer rail, like the Stander's, also helps keep you from rolling out, and pivots down when you do not need it.</p>
<h3>Fit to your mattress</h3>
<p>The listings give mattress thicknesses from 3 to 13 inches. A rail that is too loose can shift, and a gap between the rail and the mattress is the entrapment risk CPSC describes. None of these rails is for adjustable beds, according to the listings that say so.</p>
<h3>Weight limit</h3>
<p>The listed limits are 300 pounds for the Stander and the Lunderg and 380 for the KingPavonini. The Medline and Medical King listings give none in the text we read.</p>
<h3>Light</h3>
<p>The Lunderg has a motion-activated light. The CDC suggests a nightlight on the path from the bed to the bathroom.</p>

<h2>Bed Rail Questions</h2>
<h3>Are bed assist rails safe?</h3>
<p>The CPSC treats them as a regulated product because of the entrapment risk, and since August 2023 they must meet its standard. Choose a rail that names the standard, fit it as the instructions say and check the gap.</p>
<h3>Can I use a bed rail on an adjustable bed?</h3>
<p>Not with these. The Stander, Lunderg and KingPavonini listings each say the rail is not for adjustable beds, and the Stander listing adds hospital beds. An adjustable base changes the angle and the gap between mattress and rail as it moves, so a rail built for a flat frame can shift. If you have an adjustable bed, ask the maker of the rail whether it is approved for it.</p>
<h3>Does the rule cover every rail?</h3>
<p>It covers adult portable bed rails made after August 21, 2023, and CPSC's definition includes grab bars, assistive bars, transfer aids and canes used at a bed, even if the maker did not intend that use. It excludes guardrails on FDA-regulated hospital beds and children's portable bed rails, which fall under a separate rule, 16 CFR part 1224.</p>
<h3>Who should help choose one?</h3>
<p>A doctor or occupational therapist can help, especially if confusion, a risk of falling out of bed or a need for help transferring is part of the picture. They can watch how you move from lying to sitting to standing and say whether a short handle or a longer rail suits you, and whether the bed itself needs to change.</p>
<h3>What is entrapment, and how do I check for it?</h3>
<p>CPSC describes entrapment zones as areas, gaps or openings that can potentially capture or restrain a person's body part, and its standard tests four zones. For a buyer, the practical places to look are the gap between the rail and the mattress and any openings in the rail. After you install a rail, check that it does not shift and that the strap is tight, and stop using one that moves.</p>
<h3>Can I report an unsafe bed rail?</h3>
<p>Yes. CPSC's page links to saferproducts.gov, where you can report a product you think is unsafe. Keep the rail's label, which should show the maker, the model and a date of manufacture, and your Amazon order, so you can give the details. Makers and importers of rails made after August 21, 2023 must also issue a General Certificate of Compliance, which you can ask the seller for.</p>

<h2>Our Take</h2>
<p>The Stander Wonder Pivot is the best pick for anyone who wants a named standard. Its listing says it passed ASTM F3186-17, it pivots down, and 7,824 reviews average 4.6 stars. We could not read the test report, so check the label for the maker's statement.</p>
<p>If you only need a handle, the Medline has the most reviews, 15,543, but its listing names no standard and the model has been sold since 2011. The Lunderg adds a motion light that fits the CDC's nightlight advice, and the KingPavonini lists the highest limit, 380 pounds, and says it was tested to ASTM standards without naming F3186.</p>
<p>The Medical King has many reviews and the broadest claim, "meets all USA safety requirements", which names no rule. Ask the seller for its compliance certificate before you use it.</p>

<h2>The Bottom Line</h2>
<p>The Stander Wonder Pivot is the best all-round bed rail and the only one here whose listing names ASTM F3186-17. For a short handle, the Medline is the most reviewed and the Lunderg adds a night light. Check the label for the maker's compliance statement and a manufacture date after August 21, 2023, check the gap to the mattress, and never use one on an adjustable bed unless the maker says it is allowed. See also our guides to <a href="/best-bedside-commodes/">bedside commodes</a> and <a href="/best-grab-bars/">grab bars</a>.</p>'''

